    {
      "id": "c7b419f1-43da-48b2-8012-4da48912a404",
      "name": "Sticky Note f6097640",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [240, 640],
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## PLUTO — WhatsApp Channel Relay (SHARED across every branch's WhatsApp number)\nOne workflow, two entry points:\n1. WhatsApp Trigger (native n8n node) — Meta calls the app-level webhook Meta manages automatically via the WhatsApp OAuth credential; n8n handles the GET verify handshake internally, no manual IF/Respond nodes needed. It looks up the right clinic channel by phone_number_id (calling backend GET /channels?identifier=), then relays to /conversations/inbound and, if mode=ai, /chat/reply, then sends the reply straight to the Graph API using that channel's own decrypted access_token — never a static n8n credential, since every branch has its own WhatsApp number/token.\n2. Outbound Send Webhook — backend calls this (staff replies, reminders, recalls...) with {recipient, message, channel_identifier}; same per-channel token lookup, then sends via Graph API.\n\nBEFORE USE:\n- This node OWNS the Meta webhook subscription for the WhatsApp app tied to its credential. Do NOT activate any other workflow with a WhatsApp Trigger node on the same credential/app — Meta only allows one active subscriber and the last one activated wins. (This migration from a manual Webhook+verify-handshake node to this native trigger fixed exactly that bug: a stray second workflow, 'My workflow', had been silently hijacking the subscription. It has since been archived.)\n- Needs the \"PLUTO Service Token\" credential (template) wired to every \"Lookup Channel...\"/\"Send To Backend Inbound\"/\"Generate AI Reply\" HTTP Request node.\n- Update backend/app/routers/channels.py SHARED_N8N_WORKFLOWS[\"whatsapp\"] to (this workflow's id, \"whatsapp-outbound-send\")."
      }
    }
  ],
